'''Bergen-Lafayette''' is a section of [[Jersey City, New Jersey|Jersey City]], [[New Jersey]].<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.destinationjerseycity.com/districts.php |title=Jersey City's Districts |access-date=March 9, 2009 |archive-date=August 20, 2008 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080820073921/http://destinationjerseycity.com/districts.php |url-status=live }}</ref>

As its name implies, Bergen-Lafayette is made of different neighborhoods. It lies west-southwest of [[Downtown Jersey City|Downtown]] and [[Liberty State Park]]. Its less-defined other borders overlap those of [[Greenville, Jersey City|Greenville]] at [[Hudson-Bergen Light Rail]] to the south, [[Lincoln Park/West Bergen]] to the west, and Montgomery Street at  [[McGinley Square]] to the north.

The name Bergen, used throughout [[Hudson County]], is taken from the original [[Bergen, New Netherland|Bergen]], [[New Netherland]] settlement at [[Bergen Square]]. The district can correspond to the former [[Bergen City, New Jersey|Bergen City]], which existed from 1855 to 1870 and was originally incorporated as a [[town (New Jersey)|town]] by an Act of the [[New Jersey Legislature]] on March 24, 1855, from portions of [[Bergen Township, Bergen County, New Jersey (Historical 1693)|Bergen Township]]. In 1862, it did a reverse takeover, absorbing the remaining portions Bergen Township. On April 14, 1863, portions of the town were taken to form [[Greenville, Jersey City|Greenville Township]]. Bergen was reincorporated as a city on March 11, 1868.<ref name=Story>{{cite book |last=Snyder  |first=John P. |author-link= John P. Snyder |title=The Story of New Jersey's Civil Boundaries: 1606-1968 |year=1969 |publisher=Bureau of Geology and Topography | url=https://books.google.com/books?id=f4c_AAAAIAAJ }}</ref> On May 2, 1870, both Bergen City and [[Hudson City, New Jersey|Hudson City]] elected to merge with [[Jersey City, New Jersey|Jersey City]].<ref name=Story/>

==Bergen==
{{see also|Bergen Section, Jersey City}}
[[Image:BergenAve Victorians.jpg|thumb|right|Restored Victorian mansions on Bergen Avenue]]
[[County Route 501 (New Jersey)|Kennedy Boulevard]] and Bergen Avenue are a major north–south thoroughfares in the city running south from [[Journal Square]] along the ridge of the diminishing [[Palisades of the Hudson|Hudson Palisades]]. A variety of architectural styles can be found along these streets and their sidestreets including 19th-century [[rowhouse]]s, Victorian mansions, and [[Pre-war (architecture)|pre-war]] and [[Art Deco]] apartment buildings, and the [[Renaissance Revival]] former [[Jersey City YMCA]]. Monticello Avenue is a shopping district <ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.jerseycityonline.com/jersey_city_shopping_districts.htm |title=JC Shoppring Districts |access-date=August 20, 2009 |archive-date=January 19, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230119143636/http://www.jerseycityonline.com/jersey_city_shopping_districts.htm |url-status=dead }}</ref> lined with many turn-of-the-century buildings with storefronts being brought back into use. The [[Fairmount Apartments (Jersey City, New Jersey)|Fairmount Apartments]] and [[Temple Beth-El (Jersey City, New Jersey)|Temple Beth-El]] are a prominent landmark on [[County Route 501 (New Jersey)|Kennedy Boulevard]].
''[[Lincoln the Mystic]]'', a statue of a seated [[Abraham Lincoln]] by [[James Earle Fraser (sculptor)|James Earle Fraser]], is situated at its Boulevard entrance.
As its name suggests [[Lincoln Park/West Bergen|West Bergen]] overlaps this neighborhood. The city is now defining this area with maps and other promotions to encourage use of the name [[McGinley Square]].

==Beacon==
{{see also|Beacon, Jersey City}}
In 2005 the former [[Jersey City Medical Center]] was renovated and restored as [[Beacon, Jersey City|The Beacon]], with approximately 1,200 new residences. The {{convert|14|acre|m2|adj=on}} site is located at the northwest corner of the district atop [[Bergen Hill]] east of [[McGinley Square]].

==The Junction, Bergen Hill, and Jackson Hill==
{{See also|Bergen Hill, Jersey City}}
{{See also|Jackson Hill, Jersey City}}
[[File:Bergen Hill, Jersey city looking north on Grand St-Arlington Park.jpg|thumb|Grand street ascending hill at Arlington Park]]
[[Communipaw|Communipaw Junction]], or simply, The Junction, is where Communipaw, [[Summit Avenue (Hudson County)|Summit Avenue]] and [[Bergen Point Plank Road|Garfield Avenue, and Grand Street]] meet. The [[Bergen Hill|Bergen Hill Historic District]]<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.cityofjerseycity.org/docs/neighborhoods.shtml|title=Jersey City - Historic Preservation Districts|access-date=June 7, 2009|archive-date=September 4, 2009|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090904062447/http://www.cityofjerseycity.org/docs/neighborhoods.shtml|url-status=live}}</ref> is centered on Summit Avenue as it ascends from the Junction. [[Bergen Hill]], or The Hill,<ref>[http://www.cityofjerseycity.org/docs/bergen.html Bergen]{{dead link|date=October 2016 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}</ref> refers to the emergence of the [[New Jersey Palisades]]. The district is not on [[National Register of Historic Places listings in Hudson County, New Jersey|National Register of Historic Places]], but has a state designation. Library Hall is a renovated 1866 building that served as the town hall for [[Bergen Township, New Jersey (1661–1862)|Bergen Township]] then later as [[Bergen City, New Jersey|Bergen City]] before it was annexed by Jersey City in 1870. Library Hall now serves as a residential building.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://njcu.libguides.com/libraryhall|title=Library Hall/Library Hall Lofts|website=njcu.libguides.com|accessdate=July 11, 2025}}</ref> A prominent landmark is St. John's Church.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.jclandmarks.org/campaign-stjohns.shtml |title=Campaign to Protect St. John's Episcopal Church |access-date=2009-06-25 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100429094727/http://www.jclandmarks.org/campaign-stjohns.shtml |archive-date=2010-04-29 |url-status=dead }}</ref> [[Lincoln High School (Jersey City, New Jersey)|Lincoln High School]] is on Crescent Avenue. [[Ficken's Warehouse]] is on Grand Street, which as it ascends from the Junction to the [[National Register of Historic Places listings in Hudson County, New Jersey|St. Patrick's Parish and Buildings]] at Bramhall Ave. ({{Coord|40|42|50|N|74|4|23|W|display=inline}}). While not in the city historic district, this complex received its federal historic status in September 1980 and anchors the surrounding streets, some of which are lined with well-preserved or restored 18th-century row houses in [[Jackson Hill, Jersey City|Jackson Hill]]. [[The Hub, Jersey City|MLK Drive]] has long been a commercial street for the southern part of the district. The neighborhood sometimes called Claremont lies south of Arlington Park, where before discontinuation of service the [[Central Railroad of New Jersey]] maintained [[List of stations on the Central Railroad of New Jersey|station]].

==Communipaw-Lafayette==
[[File:Lafayette Park-Communipaw-Lafayette Jersey City gazebo.jpg|thumb|Gazebo at Lafayette Park]]
{{see also|Communipaw}}
Lafayette Park gives its name to the lower, or eastern, portion of the district closest to [[Downtown Jersey City]]. It is likely named for the [[Marquis de Lafayette]], who was stationed in Bergen in 1779, and later re-visited in 1824.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://njcu.libguides.com/appletree|title=Van Wagenen Homestead/Apple Tree House|work=New Jersey City University|access-date=November 12, 2025}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.cityofjerseycity.org/appletreehousejerseycity.shtml |title=Aplple Tree House |access-date=June 28, 2009 |archive-date=July 14, 2009 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090714002801/http://www.cityofjerseycity.org/appletreehousejerseycity.shtml |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>Harriet Phillips Eaton, Jersey City And Its Historic Sites, 1899:</ref> A city square similar to [[Van Vorst Park]] and [[Hamilton Park, Jersey City|Hamilton Park]] the buildings surrounding it were constructed in different periods. Before land-filling in the early twentieth century this area was located on the [[Upper New York Bay]]. The [[Hackensack (Native Americans)|Hackensack Indian]] village of [[Communipaw]] and the 16th century [[New Netherland]] plantation of [[Jan Everts Bout]], site of the [[Pavonia, New Netherland|Pavonia]] [[Kieft's War|Massacre]], were located here. [[Whitlock Cordage]]<ref name="jclandmarks">{{cite web|url=http://www.jclandmarks.org/campaign-whitlock.shtml|title=Jersey City History: The Whitlock Cordage|year=2007|publisher=The Jersey City Landmarks Conservancy|access-date=2009-06-24|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090926173942/http://www.jclandmarks.org/campaign-whitlock.shtml|archive-date=2009-09-26|url-status=dead}}</ref> is an intact complex of industrial buildings built in the Lafayette section along the banks of the [[Morris Canal]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://therealdeal.com/.../in-bergen-lafayette-a-canal-runs-through-it|title=In Bergen-Lafayette, a canal runs through it - the Real Deal}}</ref><ref>[http://www.jerseycityonline.com/ JC Online]</ref> The Housing Trust of America purchased the property to preserve the structures as affordable housing. Parts of the neighborhood are part of the [[Communipaw-Lafayette Historic District]].<ref>[http://www.state.nj.us/dep/hpo/1identify/lists/hudson.pdf NJ State Register of Historic Places in Hudson County] {{webarchive |url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100705114811/http://www.state.nj.us/dep/hpo/1identify/lists/hudson.pdf |date=July 5, 2010 }}</ref>  [[Berry Lane Park]], which upon completion will be the largest municipal park in Jersey City, is under construction along Garfield Avenue.

==Transportation==
The [[Liberty State Park (HBLR station)|Liberty State Park]], [[Garfield Avenue (HBLR station)|Garfield Avenue]], and [[Martin Luther King Drive (HBLR station)|Martin Luther King]] stations of the [[Hudson-Bergen Light Rail]] are on the periphery of the section. [[List of New Jersey Transit bus routes (1–99)|New Jersey Transit bus routes 6, 8, 16, 81, 87]] to [[Journal Square]], [[Exchange Place (Jersey City)|Exchange Place]], and [[Hoboken Terminal]] serve the area locally.

